   "name": "Countrysides in Africa: agricultural landscapes and rural settlements"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "The countryside (rural area) is the land outside towns, where most people farm.\nAgricultural landscapes:\n• forest zone: small food-crop farms in clearings (shifting cultivation, bush fallowing) and plantations of cocoa, coffee, oil palm, rubber and bananas (the CDC around Mount Cameroon);\n• highlands: terraced or ridged fields, mixed farming with cattle (bocage hedges in the West);\n• savanna: large open fields of millet, sorghum, cotton and groundnuts, and grazing land.\nRural settlements:\n• dispersed (scattered): houses far apart on each farm, as in the Western Highlands;\n• nucleated (clustered): houses grouped together, for defence or around water;\n• linear: houses along a road, river or railway, as in the South and East.\nBuilding materials vary: mud bricks, thatch, wood, bamboo, zinc roofs.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "s": "houses built in a line along a road"
  },
  "problem": "What type of rural settlement has {s}, and why?",
  "steps": [
   "The houses follow the road, one after another.",
   "People live near the road for transport and trade."
  ],
  "answer": "A linear settlement.",
  "checks": []
 },
